[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Java Streams API: Intermediate Operations, Terminal Operations, and Collectors"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Java Streams API, introduced in Java 8, revolutionised how developers process collections of data. By leveraging functional programming paradigms, streams enable declarative data processing, making code more readable, concise, and easier to parallelise."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Anatomy of a Stream Pipeline"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A stream pipeline consists of three fundamental parts: a data source (such as a List or Set), zero or more intermediate operations, and a single terminal operation. Without a terminal operation, intermediate operations do not execute, as streams are lazy by design."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"List<String> names = List.of("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie", "Anna");

long count = names.stream()
    .filter(name -> name.startsWith("A")) // Intermediate operation
    .map(String::toUpperCase)           // Intermediate operation
    .count();                           // Terminal operation

System.out.println(count); // Output: 2"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Intermediate Operations"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Intermediate operations return another Stream, allowing you to chain multiple operations together. They are lazy, meaning they delay execution until a terminal operation is invoked."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"filter(Predicate<T> predicate): Returns a stream consisting of elements that match the given predicate."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"map(Function<T, R> mapper): Transforms each element using the provided function."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"sorted(): Sorts the stream elements in natural order or via a custom Comparator."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"distinct(): Removes duplicate elements based on the equals() method."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Terminal Operations"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Terminal operations trigger the execution of the pipeline and produce a result or a side-effect. Once a terminal operation is performed, the stream is considered consumed and cannot be reused."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"forEach(Consumer<T> action): Performs an action for each element of the stream."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"reduce(BinaryOperator<T> accumulator): Combines stream elements into a single reduced value."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"anyMatch / allMatch / noneMatch: Short-circuiting operations returning a boolean based on a predicate."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Powerful Reduction with Collectors"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Collectors class provides a vast repository of reduction operations, commonly used with the collect() terminal operation. Collectors can transform streams into lists, sets, maps, or perform complex groupings and statistical summaries."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"List<String> fruits = List.of("apple", "banana", "apple", "orange", "banana", "apple");

Map<String, Long> fruitCountMap = fruits.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Function.identity(),
        Collectors.counting()
    ));

System.out.println(fruitCountMap); 
// Output: {banana=2, orange=1, apple=3}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering intermediate operations, terminal operations, and the Collectors utility, you can write expressive, efficient, and maintainable data processing pipelines in modern Java applications."}]
